          "inlines": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "A set of numbers is an ",
              "marks": []
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "arithmetic progression",
              "marks": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": " if the difference between successive terms is constant. For example, ",
              "marks": []
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "latex": "\\{1,4,7\\}",
              "display": false
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": " is an arithmetic progression with common difference 3.",
              "marks": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "p19-q13-given",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "fontSize": null,
          "align": "left",
          "spaceBefore": 0,
          "spaceAfter": 2,
          "lineHeight": 1.4,
          "indent": 0,
          "inlines": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The polynomial equation ",
              "marks": []
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "latex": "x^3-3x^2-x+3=0",
              "display": false
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": " has its roots in arithmetic progression.",
              "marks": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p19-q13-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Let the roots be $(\\alpha-d)$, $\\alpha$ and $(\\alpha+d)$, where $d$ is the common difference.\nUse sum of roots to find a root.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\alpha=1$.",
          "worked": "Using the sum of roots:\n$$\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha-d)+\\alpha+(\\alpha+d)&=3\\\\3\\alpha&=3\\\\\\alpha&=1\\end{aligned}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 18
      },
      {
        "id": "p19-q13-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Write down the corresponding linear factor, then completely factorise $x^3-3x^2-x+3=0$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Linear factor: $x-1$; $(x-1)(x-3)(x+1)=0$.",
          "worked": "Since 1 is a root, $x-1$ is a linear factor. Long division gives\n$$\\begin{array}{r|rrrr} &x^2-2x-3&&&\\\\x-1&x^3&-3x^2&-x&+3\\\\ &x^3&-x^2&&\\\\\\hline &&-2x^2&-x&\\\\ &&-2x^2&+2x&\\\\\\hline &&&-3x&+3\\\\ &&&-3x&+3\\\\\\hline &&&&0\\end{array}$$\n$$\\begin{aligned}x^3-3x^2-x+3&=(x-1)(x^2-2x-3)\\\\&=(x-1)(x-3)(x+1)\\end{aligned}$$\nHence the equation is $(x-1)(x-3)(x+1)=0$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 33
      },
      {
        "id": "p19-q13-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "Therefore, find the roots of $x^3-3x^2-x+3=0$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$x=-1,1,3$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{aligned}(x-1)(x-3)(x+1)&=0\\\\x&=1,\\ 3,\\ -1\\end{aligned}$$\nThe roots are $-1,1,3$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 14
      }
    ]
  },
